# Seaport byte ledger

The Fe port of Seaport 1.6 (`cburgdorf/SeaportFe`) compiles to **65,222** runtime bytes. The EIP-170 limit is
24,576, so it is **40,646 bytes over**. We traced every byte back through Fe and Sonatina to see where the size comes
from and which changes could move it.

Setup: Fe `342048ea5` with the port's compiler integration patch, Sonatina `3357f3a2`, `fe build -O 1` (the port's
setting). The build reproduces the port's published sizes byte for byte, and its Foundry suite passes 482 of 482.

## Summary

- **Optimizer changes move Seaport by 3% at most.** #344 changes nothing on it. `-O 1` and `-O 2` share the IR
  pipeline, so Seaport apparently has none of the phi ladders it targets. The smallest build we measured is Sonatina
  main plus two ported passes at `-O s`: 63,266 bytes.
- **Per operation, Fe's code looks like solc's.**
  - Stack shuffles (DUP, SWAP, POP) are 20.1% of Fe's bytes, against 19.9% and 22.4% for the two solc builds.
  - PUSH bytes are 53 to 57% in all three.
  - On the periphery contracts, Fe is 0.92 to 1.23 times the size of the plain Solidity reference build. SeaportRouter,
    which has no reference build, is 1.55 times the deployed one.
  - Seaport alone is 2.6 times.
- **Memory traffic is the outlier:** 6,773 memory opcodes against about 1,150 in solc, nearly six times as many.
  - About 60% come from the backend: spills, call transport, stack objects and other inserted code.
  - 37% come from Fe's lowering: aggregate copies, loads and stores, and allocation.
- **Sharing repeated code would save about 8 to 10 KB** after call costs, measured under three comparison rules.
  Useful, but a quarter of what is needed.
- **The largest single items** are backend spill traffic (6,750 bytes), seven `decode_from_bounded` instances (5,553
  bytes) and 123 free-pointer clamps (2,091 bytes).
- **The port's own code is not the problem.**
  - The 84 functions that pair by name with seaport-core come to 16,694 bytes in Fe against 16,023 in solc's plain
    build.
  - Nearly the whole gap is outside those pairs: library ABI code, dispatch, generated glue and code with no source
    link, 48,528 bytes against 9,111.
- **Against plain Solidity, named causes explain 59% of the excess** by measurement, and an estimate brings that to
  about 92%. See the excess ledger below.
  - Removing all six measured causes would still leave about 41.7 KB.
  - Most of the remainder is how calls and structs are passed through memory, plus backend glue.

