## Excess against plain Solidity

Fe's Seaport minus solc's plain reference `Consideration` is 40,088 bytes. The same classifier runs on both sides.
Every byte of each runtime sits in exactly one bucket, so the differences add up exactly. An independent rebuild of the
ledgers matches.

**Measured:**

| Cause | Fe | solc | Excess |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| ABI decoding and encoding | 13,107 | 4,896 | 8,211 |
| Spill stores and reloads (Sonatina, Fe only) | 6,750 | 0 | 6,750 |
| Repeated code beyond its first copy (16 bytes or more) | 7,767 | 3,765 | 4,002 |
| Free-pointer clamp at each heap allocation (Fe only) | 2,091 | 0 | 2,091 |
| Identical functions left unmerged (Fe only) | 1,330 | 0 | 1,330 |
| Checked arithmetic | 1,431 | 296 | 1,135 |
| **Named causes (58.7%)** | | | **23,519** |
| Leftover, grouped only by what the instructions do (41.3%) | | | 16,569 |

solc repeats code too, so the repeated-code row counts only Fe's extra. Sharing all of Fe's repeats would save more (8
to 10 KB, above), but part of that would also be possible in solc's build.

The leftover is mostly memory address constants (5,557), control flow (3,655), stack shuffles (2,956) and memory
opcodes (2,377).

**Estimated, not measured:** splitting the leftover in proportion to what Fe emitted those bytes for:

| Mechanism | Bytes, about |
|---|---:|
| Call arguments and returns passed through memory | 4,200 |
| Backend code with no IR origin | 3,500 |
| Aggregate copies | 3,400 |
| Stack objects | 1,200 |
| No mechanism label at all | 3,000 (7.6%) |

**Grouped by who could fix it:**
- **Sonatina:** about 19 KB. Spills, the clamp and the unmerged identical functions are measured, 10.2 KB. Call
  transport, backend code with no IR origin, and stack objects are estimated, 8.9 KB.
- **Fe lowering and library:** about 12.7 KB, from ABI decoding and encoding, aggregate copies and checked arithmetic.
- **Either side:** 4.0 KB of repeated code, by outlining in Sonatina or by sharing in Fe.

**Two more results:**
- Ignoring types on Sonatina IR finds nothing beyond exact matches. The `decode_from_bounded` instances differ in
  structure (one field walk per layout), so a single generic decoder would need runtime layout descriptors.
- Fe and solc share no basic block of 16 bytes or more under any comparison rule. The two compilers' code does not
  line up block for block.

## How to fix it

Levers ranked by size. "Est." marks an estimated size; the others are measured.

| Lever | About | Who | Status |
|---|---:|---|---|
| Read ABI fields from calldata when needed, instead of decoding whole structs into memory. The per-layout copies differ in their field walks, so one generic decoder would need runtime layout descriptors. | 8.2 KB | Fe std and lowering | design question |
| Fewer spills from stackify | 6.8 KB | Sonatina | #339 shares spill words; all of main at `ee325c3b` moved Seaport by 17 bytes |
| Pass call arguments and returns on the stack rather than through memory | 4.2 KB, est. | Sonatina | none yet |
| Avoid aggregate copies (pass references, move values) | 3.4 KB, est. | Fe | none yet |
| Backend code with no IR origin | 3.5 KB, est. | Sonatina | needs provenance first to say what it is |
| The free-pointer clamp: carry the floor proof forward, or share one clamped-allocation routine | 2.1 KB | Sonatina | none yet; small |
| Merge identical functions at the IR level | 1.3 KB | Sonatina | none yet |
| Guarded subtraction proof and return-value removal | 0.5 KB | Sonatina | ready on a local branch |
| In the port: build `env` once or pass a reference (0.8 KB); build at `-O s` (1.3 KB) | 2.1 KB | the port | easy |

**Can it get under 24,576?** It needs 40,646 bytes off. Even if every row above were fully removable, which they are not,
the total is about 33 KB. Getting under the limit probably needs the calldata-view change plus several of the Sonatina
fixes, and may still fall short. That matches how seaport-core fits only with hand-written calldata-pointer assembly.
Splitting the contract would be the fallback, but that changes the port's design.

**Related PRs.** Already included in what we measured:
- #1618, which shares runtime instances across call sites and took Seaport from 154 KB to 65 KB;
- #339 and #340;
- #344, which does nothing on Seaport.

Merged since and not yet measured: #353 (lazy dynamic frames) and #349 (phi-of-constants folding). Open and not yet
measured: #317 (EVM switches as cost-based comparison trees), which could affect the dispatcher: control flow is 3.7
KB of the excess.

